Below is a copy-paste-ready master prompt for creating your local AI coding assistant.

```text
You are a senior software architect and developer. Design and implement a fully local, privacy-first AI coding assistant similar in workflow to Claude Code, but built with Java and Spring Boot.

The assistant must help with:

- Code analysis
- Debugging
- Error explanation
- Refactoring
- Feature planning
- Architecture design
- Test generation
- Documentation
- Code review
- Repository exploration
- Multi-file changes
- Git-aware development workflows
- General technical thinking and problem solving

## Core technical constraints

Use the following technology stack:

- Java
- Spring Boot
- llama.cpp Java binding from de.kherud
- GGUF models already available on the local machine
- Maven or Gradle, but choose one and remain consistent

Strictly obey these constraints:

1. Do not call any third-party API.
2. Do not use OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, Azure, Hugging Face APIs, or similar services.
3. Do not download models or any other internet resources.
4. The application must work completely offline.
5. Only load GGUF models from user-configured local filesystem paths.
6. Do not use internet-based dependency resolution during runtime.
7. Do not upload source code, prompts, logs, repository files, or user data anywhere.
8. Do not silently send telemetry or analytics.
9. Do not create unnecessary files on disk.
10. Do not save generated responses, intermediate prompts, repository copies, embeddings, indexes, or chat history to disk unless the user explicitly requests it.
11. Avoid writing temporary files during inference. Prefer in-memory processing.
12. If temporary files are unavoidable, use bounded-size temporary files and delete them immediately after use.
13. Never duplicate an entire repository in memory or on disk.
14. Use streaming, pagination, file-size limits, and bounded buffers to prevent memory and disk exhaustion.

## Main product goal

Build a command-line-first local coding assistant with an optional Spring Boot service layer.

The primary interface should feel similar to a local version of Claude Code:

- The user can open a repository.
- The user can register or tag repositories.
- The user can ask questions about one repository or several repositories.
- The assistant can inspect files, search code, explain architecture, plan changes, and optionally modify files.
- The user must explicitly approve file modifications and dangerous commands.
- The assistant should maintain useful context without storing large persistent data.

## Repository management

Implement repository registration using filesystem paths instead of copying repositories.

Example commands:

```text
codeai repo add my-project /home/user/projects/my-project
codeai repo list
codeai repo remove my-project
codeai repo tag my-project backend java spring
codeai repo untag my-project spring
codeai repo info my-project
codeai use my-project
```

Repository metadata should be lightweight. Store only minimal metadata such as:

- Repository name
- Absolute path
- Tags
- Optional description
- Optional preferred language
- Optional build command
- Optional test command
- Optional run command

Do not index or duplicate every file by default.

If persistent metadata is necessary, use a small local configuration file or embedded lightweight database. Make persistence optional and clearly documented.

## Supported languages

The assistant must support at least:

- Java
- Python
- HTML
- CSS
- JavaScript
- TypeScript
- Node.js
- JSON
- YAML
- XML
- SQL
- Bash
- Markdown
- Dockerfile
